// Debounce -- accept a noisy digital signal only once it has held still.
//
// A mechanical contact rings for a few milliseconds after every transition,
// and a rule that reads the pin directly sees every one of those edges. This
// makes the signal usable:
//
//   #Debounce btn  T.period=20
//   btn.In = !Button
//   Led = btn.Out
//
// The settle time is the timer's period, so it is set with the `.part` form
// at instantiation -- and it stays patchable in the field, which is what you
// want when a switch turns out to be worse than the datasheet claimed.
//
#module Debounce
#timer    T 50                    // settle time in ms; override with T.period=
#variable In:1   unsigned in  = 0 // raw, bouncing
#variable Out:1  unsigned out = 0 // settled value
#variable Prev:1 unsigned     = 0 // the value we last accepted

// Hold the settle timer restarted for as long as the raw signal disagrees
// with what we have accepted. It can only run out once the input has stopped
// moving for a whole period, so every extra bounce pushes the decision
// further away and a ringing contact simply never gets a verdict.
T = 1 ? In != Prev
Prev = In, Out = In ? timeout(T)
#end
